---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "/";
const source = "https://github.com/lordkeremello45/HWcontrol2.0";
const releases = source + "/releases";
const productSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
  name: "HWcontrol2.0",
  applicationCategory: "UtilitiesApplication",
  operatingSystem: "Windows 10/11 (x64), macOS 14+ (Apple Silicon), Debian/Ubuntu-family Linux (x64; hardware access varies)",
  description: "Open-source beta desktop software for hardware monitoring, diagnostics and capability-gated device control. Availability and device support depend on the operating system, drivers, firmware and hardware.",
  softwareVersion: "Beta",
  license: "https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.html",
  url: "https://lordkeremello45.github.io/HWcontrol2.0/",
  downloadUrl: releases,
  codeRepository: source,
  isAccessibleForFree: true
};
---
<BaseLayout title="HWcontrol2.0 Product Overview | HWcontrol Team" description="Explore HWcontrol2.0 hardware monitoring, diagnostics, supported control paths, beta release channels, privacy model and platform limitations." active="product">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(productSchema)} />
  <header class="page-header">
    <span class="eyebrow">Flagship desktop product</span>
    <h1>HWcontrol2.0</h1>
    <p class="lead">An open-source desktop platform for hardware monitoring, diagnostics and supported device control across Windows, macOS and Linux.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href={releases}>Open GitHub Releases ↗</a>
      <a class="button" href={source}>Review source code</a>
      <a class="button" href={`${base}getting-started/`}>Installation guide</a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="notice"><p><strong>Beta status:</strong> HWcontrol2.0 is still beta software. Read the release notes and check that the expected package assets exist before downloading. Supported controls vary by OS, drivers, firmware and hardware; unsupported paths must remain monitor-only.</p></div>
  <section class="section grid">
    <article class="card"><h2>Monitor</h2><p>CPU, memory, storage, GPU and fan telemetry where the operating system and driver expose a reliable source. Temperature history and alerts help users observe changes.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h2>Control carefully</h2><p>Profiles and fan-curve workflows are capability-gated. The UI should not imply that a device can be controlled when its backend does not support that operation.</p></article>
    <article class="card"><h2>Diagnose locally</h2><p>Local diagnostics, health states and exportable reports are designed to help investigate issues without requiring routine behavioral tracking.</p></article>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">Release targets</span><h2>Choose the correct platform</h2><p class="muted">These are intended beta targets; the current release page is the source of truth for available artifacts.</p></div>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Platform</th><th>Target</th><th>Expected packages</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Windows</td><td>Windows 10/11, x64</td><td>Guided EXE, MSI, MSIX, portable ZIP</td><td>Use the guided installer for typical installations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>macOS</td><td>macOS 14+, Apple Silicon</td><td>PKG, DMG containing .app</td><td>Intel macOS is not currently a shipped target.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Linux</td><td>Debian/Ubuntu-family, x64</td><td>DEB, TAR.GZ, TAR.ZST</td><td>Hardware access depends on kernel, drivers and permissions.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>
  <section class="section split-section">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">Trust boundaries</span><h2>Local-first by design</h2><p class="muted">The dashboard communicates with an authenticated localhost Go bridge, which mediates access to a native C++ engine and platform-specific APIs. The bridge is a security boundary, not a reason to assume every OS or hardware path is equally supported.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}security/`}>Read security notes →</a></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Before installing</h3><ul class="compact-list"><li>Read the release notes and known limitations.</li><li>Verify SHA-256 files when published with the release.</li><li>Remember that a checksum is not publisher signing.</li><li>Back up important data before testing beta hardware-control software.</li><li>Report reproducible issues with secrets removed from logs.</li></ul></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
